Reproduced against `3c1f8cab89d851d9dc4cc8e5da86d8ac8874e3d0` (fetched main), Node 24.19.0, with the repository lockfile and SDK 2026.11.4. Only synthetic input and temporary local trackers were used.

The secret guard rejects `password=SyntheticOnly123!` but accepts `{"password":"SyntheticOnly123!"}`. The latter is stored as durable memory and copied into an agents-md export.

Source:
- [The assignment matcher permits whitespace, but no closing quote, between the credential key and its separator](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-memory/blob/3c1f8cab89d851d9dc4cc8e5da86d8ac8874e3d0/src/core.ts#L40-L49).
- [remember calls this guard before persistence](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-memory/blob/3c1f8cab89d851d9dc4cc8e5da86d8ac8874e3d0/src/memory.ts#L16-L24).

Reproduction:

```sh
review=$(mktemp -d)
git clone --quiet https://github.com/unbraind/pm-memory.git "$review/package"
cd "$review/package"
git checkout --quiet 3c1f8cab89d851d9dc4cc8e5da86d8ac8874e3d0
npm ci --ignore-scripts --no-audit --no-fund
npm run build
cat > repro-secret.mjs <<'JS'
import assert from 'node:assert/strict';
import { mkdtemp, readFile, readdir } from 'node:fs/promises';
import { tmpdir } from 'node:os';
import { join } from 'node:path';
import { PmClient } from '@unbrained/pm-cli/sdk';
import { remember, get, exportDir } from './dist/index.js';
const root = await mkdtemp(join(tmpdir(), 'memory-review-'));
const workspace = join(root, 'tracker');
const pm = new PmClient({ cwd: root, pmRoot: workspace, author: 'fixture', noExtensions: true });
await pm.init('fixture', { defaults: true, agentGuidance: 'skip', mergeFence: false });
await pm.config('project', 'set', 'agent_identity.probes_enabled', 'false');
const options = { workspace, author: 'fixture', kind: 'reference' };
try { await remember('password=SyntheticOnly123!', { ...options, slug: 'plain' }); console.log('plain=accepted'); }
catch (e) { assert.equal(e.code, 'privacy'); console.log('plain=privacy'); }
const fact = '{"password":"SyntheticOnly123!"}';
await remember(fact, { ...options, slug: 'quoted' });
const saved = await get('quoted', options);
assert.equal(saved.body, fact);
console.log('quoted=accepted; stored_verbatim=true');
await exportDir(join(root, 'export'), { workspace, author: 'fixture', to: 'agents-md' });
async function allText(dir) { let value = ''; for (const e of await readdir(dir, { withFileTypes: true })) value += e.isDirectory() ? await allText(join(dir, e.name)) : await readFile(join(dir, e.name), 'utf8'); return value; }
const exported = await allText(join(root, 'export'));
console.log('export_contains_literal=' + exported.includes('SyntheticOnly123!'));
JS
node repro-secret.mjs
```

Observed output:

```text
plain=privacy
quoted=accepted; stored_verbatim=true
export_contains_literal=true
```

Expected: quoted JSON credential assignments receive the same typed privacy refusal as equivalent unquoted assignments, before any item/history write.

Actual: the quote after the key prevents the assignment expression from matching; ordinary passwords without a provider-specific token prefix pass every remaining check. The public SDK accepts and persists the entire JSON string.

Impact: copied JSON configuration or API payloads can bypass the advertised credential refusal and carry secrets into durable history and portable agent context. The reproduction uses an intentionally synthetic password, not a live credential. This is independent of SDK admission/adoption work.

Suggested direction: cover quoted credential keys in the bounded scanner, or inspect bounded structured input without losing the free-text guard. Add positive refusal controls for JSON and quoted YAML keys, including password and api_key, and verify no durable write occurs. Keep accepted secret-manager/environment references as explicit negative controls.

Duplicate check: searched all issue states for privacy, secret, quoted, and password; no matching issue was found.
